**Read:** [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), stability and phase-equilibrium chapters; [Advanced Thermodynamics — MIT 2.43, Spring 2024](../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC), metastability and spinodal decomposition; [The physics and mathematics of the second law — Lieb and Yngvason](../sources/Second-Law-Foundations-Lieb-Yngvason.lingua#r_4ZBR10NAYWD4G8ZJYQV7PXS8DZ), equilibrium assumptions and admissible states.

An equilibrium state is stable only with respect to the allowed perturbations and constraints. Entropy maximization or the corresponding potential minimum constrains response functions and curvature. Establish the variables and ensemble-like contact conditions before interpreting a Hessian.

Separate local stability, global phase preference and kinetic persistence. A metastable superheated liquid may be locally sustained until nucleation, while a spinodal instability is a loss of the relevant local stability. A polynomial equation of state can supply several mathematical roots, not all physically selected.

Construct a free-energy-versus-composition example and compare local curvature with a common-tangent construction. Follow how susceptibility, compressibility or capacity changes near a transition. Discuss finite-size, nonequilibrium and experimental-resolution limits before interpreting an apparent divergence.

